questions.modelAnswer:
Run exactly one query against incident filtered to active=true, and build a department-to-count map in memory as you loop through the results once:

var counts = {};
var inc = new GlideRecord('incident');
inc.addQuery('active', true);
inc.query();
while (inc.next()) {
    var deptId = inc.getValue('department');
    counts[deptId] = (counts[deptId] || 0) + 1;
}
var depts = new GlideRecord('cmn_department');
depts.addQuery('active', true);
depts.query();
while (depts.next()) {
    gs.info(depts.getValue('name') + ': ' + (counts[depts.getUniqueValue()] || 0));
}

This issues exactly two queries total — one against incident, one against cmn_department — no matter whether there are 400 departments or 40,000. The original pattern issues one query per department inside the loop, so its total query count grows linearly with the number of departments (N+1); the rewrite moves all the counting into an in-memory JavaScript object after a single incident query, so department count has zero effect on how many queries run.

